course: biology
lesson: 62
title: Fungi: mushrooms, mould and yeast
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A mushroom is not a plant and not an animal: it belongs to a third great kingdom of life, the fungi, which also includes moulds and the yeast in bread.
explain: The mushroom you see is only the fruit; the real fungus is a hidden web of threads, the mycelium, spread through the soil or a log like a giant tangle of white cotton.
explain: After rain on the park lawn, a ring of mushrooms can pop up overnight: the threads have been growing outwards in a circle for years, and the mushrooms appear at the edge, the «fairy ring» of old stories.
explain: Fungi cannot make food from sunlight; they feed by breathing out juices that break down food around them and then soaking it up, which is why they rot dead leaves, logs and the forgotten orange in the fruit bowl.
explain: Without fungi forests would drown in dead wood, bread would be flat, and the first antibiotic would never have been found; but some are deadly poisonous, so wild mushrooms are for looking at, never for eating.
fact: Fungi are their own kingdom of life, separate from plants and animals; they have no green chlorophyll and cannot make food from sunlight, and they are in fact closer relatives of animals than of plants.
fact: A mushroom is only the fruiting part of a fungus; the main body is mycelium, a web of fine threads in the soil or wood, and a fairy ring forms where the threads grow outwards in a circle.
fact: Fungi spread by spores, tiny specks like dust; a giant puffball can hold trillions of them, and a raindrop or a foot makes them puff out like smoke; most spores land in bad places, so only a few grow into new fungi.
fact: The red toadstool with white spots, the fly agaric, is poisonous, and the death cap, which looks plain and harmless, can kill; nobody should ever eat a wild mushroom unless an expert has checked it.
fact: Mould is a fungus too; on an old orange or a slice of bread its threads grow far inside the food and the green or blue fuzz on top is made of spores.
fact: In 1928 Alexander Fleming came back from a holiday to a messy lab and, instead of throwing out a spoiled dish, looked closely and saw that a mould called Penicillium had killed the bacteria around it on a dish; it made penicillin, which other scientists turned into a medicine in the 1940s, saving millions of lives.
fact: Yeast is a fungus made of single tiny cells; in bread dough it eats sugar and gives off carbon dioxide gas, whose bubbles make the dough rise; yeast works fast when warm and slowly in the cold.
fact: Most land plants have fungi joined to their roots, called mycorrhiza, which pass water and minerals to the plant and get sugars from it in return.
fact: Fungi are decomposers: bracket fungi on a fallen log break down the hard wood slowly and return its goodness to the soil.
fact: A honey fungus in the Blue Mountains of Oregon, USA, spreads underground over about 9 square kilometres and is thought to be thousands of years old, perhaps the biggest living thing on Earth.
fact: Some fungi glow in the dark with a soft green light, called bioluminescence, made by a chemical reaction inside them, like the ghost fungus of Australia.
fact: Athlete's foot is a fungus that grows on warm, damp skin, often between the toes; drying feet well and wearing flip-flops at the pool help keep it away.
exercise: Which kingdom of life do mushrooms belong to? | The fungi, a kingdom of their own | The plants, like trees, grass and ferns | The animals, like snails and slugs | The bacteria, like the germs in old milk
exercise: Why can't a fungus make food from sunlight? | It has no green chlorophyll | It lives and grows only at night | It has no roots down in the soil | It is too small to catch light
exercise: Fungi are closer relatives of which group? | Animals | Green plants | Rocks in the soil | Bacteria
exercise: What is the main body of a fungus? | A hidden web of threads called mycelium | The cap and stalk of the mushroom you can see | A single big seed under the ground | A long root like a carrot's
exercise: Why do mushrooms in a fairy ring grow in a circle? | The threads underground grow outwards and fruit at the edge | Fairies or rabbits dance round and round in circles there at night | The wind blows the spores in a ring | Rain falls in circles on the lawn
exercise: What comes out when a puffball is squeezed? | A cloud of tiny spores | Smoke from a fire inside | Poison gas that stings your eyes | Water full of tiny seeds
exercise: What should you do with a bright red toadstool with white spots in a forest? | Leave it alone, as it is poisonous | Pick it and fry it for your tea | Taste a little to check if it is safe | Feed it to your dog
exercise: What is the green fuzz on an old orange? | Mould, a fungus whose spores make the colour | Dust that settled from the air over a week | The orange's own juice drying out | Tiny green insects eating it
exercise: What did Alexander Fleming notice in 1928? | A mould on his dish had killed the bacteria around it | That bread rises when it is warm | That some mushrooms glow green in the dark | That bacteria can be seen with a magnifying glass
exercise: What medicine came from Fleming's mould? | Penicillin, the first antibiotic | Aspirin, for headaches | The first vaccine, for smallpox | Insulin, for people with diabetes
exercise: What makes bread dough rise? | Yeast giving off carbon dioxide bubbles | Flour swelling up with water | Air pushed in by the baker's hands as she kneads | Salt reacting with heat
exercise: What are the white threads around tree roots in the soil? | Fungi that trade water and minerals for the tree's sugars | Baby roots of the grass | Worm trails filled with slime that the worms leave behind them | Spider webs under the ground
exercise: What job do bracket fungi do on a fallen log? | They break down the dead wood and return it to the soil | They keep the log alive and growing new branches from the side | They make the log harder | They protect the log from rotting
exercise: What may be the biggest living thing on Earth? | A honey fungus spreading about 9 square kilometres in Oregon | A blue whale in the Pacific | A giant redwood tree in California, over a hundred metres tall | A coral reef fish shoal
exercise: Why do some mushrooms glow green in the dark? | A chemical reaction inside them makes light | They soak up moonlight and glow | They are covered in tiny glow-worms | They are radioactive
exercise: What causes athlete's foot? | A fungus growing on warm, damp skin | Running too fast in races on hard ground | Too much salt in the swimming pool water | A bee sting between the toes in summer
test: My sister says mushrooms are just plants without leaves. Right? | No, fungi are their own kingdom and can't make food from sunlight | Yes, they are plants that lost their leaves long ago and now grow in the dark | Yes, they are baby trees | No, they are animals that stay still
test: In a cartoon, the hero eats a red toadstool with white spots and grows huge. What is the truth? | The fly agaric is poisonous and must never be eaten | It is safe and gives you strength, like spinach | It only makes you sleepy | It is safe when cooked for long enough
test: My grandad picked some plain white mushrooms in the field, they look fine innit. Safe? | Not unless an expert checks them, as deadly ones can look plain | Yes, white ones are always safe | Yes, if they smell nice | Yes, the poisonous ones are always brightly coloured to warn you off
test: What would happen to a forest if every fungus disappeared? | Dead wood and leaves would pile up and many trees would grow badly | Trees would grow twice as fast without any fungi stealing their sugar | Nothing would change at all | The forest would fill with mushrooms
test: You leave bread dough in the fridge instead of a warm place. What will happen? | It will rise more slowly, as the yeast works slowly in the cold | It will rise faster than ever, because yeast loves the cold best of all | It will turn into mould at once | It will never change at all
test: Fleming only noticed the mould because he came back from holiday to a messy dish. What does that teach? | Careful scientists look at surprises instead of throwing them away | Messy labs always find medicines, so you should never clean up at all | Holidays make bacteria die | Mould only grows when people are away
test: ew, mate cut the green bit off the bread and ate the rest. fine yeah? | Better not, the mould's threads can reach far inside the bread | Yes, mould only ever lives on the outside, so cutting it off is fine | Yes, mould is just a vitamin | Yes, toasting it removes all mould
test: In a video game a glowing mushroom is a magic potion. Why do real ones glow? | A chemical reaction inside makes light, called bioluminescence | They are full of magic | They store up the sunlight from the day and let it out slowly at night | They are covered in lightning bugs
test: After swimming, Leo's toes are itchy and flaky. What is the best advice? | Dry between the toes well and wear flip-flops at the pool | Put your feet in salty water every night before bed for a week | Run more to toughen the skin | Keep wet socks on to soothe it
test: Why does a fairy ring get bigger every year? | The fungus threads keep growing outwards | Fairies dance in a bigger ring every year | More rain falls on the lawn | Mushrooms walk outwards at night
test: A puffball the size of a football bursts. Why does it make so many spores? | Most spores land in bad places, so only a few grow into new fungi | Every spore always grows into a new puffball | To make clouds and rain | To feed birds that eat dust
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the organisms known as fungi? | They constitute a separate kingdom and obtain food by absorption | They are green plants deficient in leaves | They are microscopic animals | They manufacture food from sunlight by means of a green pigment in the cap
exam: Identify the principal function of yeast in the leavening of bread. | The production of carbon dioxide gas from sugar | The absorption of water by flour | The neutralisation of salt | The hardening of the crust in the oven
exam: It is given that an individual has collected wild mushrooms of unknown kind. Select the course of action consistent with established safety principles. | They should not be consumed unless identified by an expert | They may be consumed if white in colour | They may be consumed if cooked thoroughly at a high temperature | They may be consumed if pleasant in odour
exam: Ascertain the substance discovered by Alexander Fleming in 1928. | Penicillin, produced by a mould | Insulin, produced by the pancreas | Yeast, employed in baking | A vaccine against smallpox
